> That indeed is a problem. Not sure what the right fix is for that though.

The way to make sure transitivity is guaranteed is to always compare a property and only fall back to the next property if they are *equal*. If only one has the property you simply (arbitrarily) define which one goes first (in this example, that's the job of the cmp function):

```
r = cmp(sort-key-a, sort-key-b);
if (r != 0)
    return r;

/* Both either have a key or both don't */

if (sort-key-a) {
    r = cmp(machine-id-a, machine-id-b);
    if (r != 0)
        return r;
    r = cmp(version-a, version-b);
    if (r != 0)
        return r;
}

/* Any further backup cmp. */
```

Also, I still don't like the whole approach as you either still have to maintain the "lower to higher" priority ordering or otherwise you can end up mixing the old stuff "older/broken first, newer last" vs the new stuff with a sort-key "newer first, older/broken last". And, btw, you this still doesn't solve the question of how we select the default entry by pattern: entries without sort-key want the last entry matching, those with one want the very first matching.

Btw, have I mention I hate sorting by filenames when it should be done by content (this applies to userspace too)?

